### Deep Study: Thread Creation Mechanisms in Java with Concurrent Execution

#### 1. Detailed Architectural Comparison:
| Parameter | Extending `Thread` Class | Implementing `Runnable` Interface |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Inheritance** | Consumes Java's single inheritance capability; subclass cannot inherit any other domain base class. | Preserves class inheritance; subclass can extend any parent class while implementing `Runnable`. |
| **Separation of Concerns** | Violates SRP (Single Responsibility Principle): task logic and thread lifecycle are tightly coupled. | Adheres to SRP: the class represents a task (`Runnable`), while `Thread` handles execution. |
| **Resource Sharing** | Each thread object has its own separate memory footprint. | A single `Runnable` instance can be shared across multiple concurrent `Thread` objects. |
| **Thread Pool Compatibility** | Poor: Worker threads in a pool cannot easily reuse thread objects. | Native: Directly accepted by `ExecutorService`, `Callable`, and modern concurrency frameworks. |

#### 2. University Examination Problem Solution:
> *Problem:* Write a complete Java program demonstrating concurrent execution of two threads (one printing even numbers and one printing odd numbers) using `Runnable`, ensuring the main thread waits until both complete.

```java
class NumberPrinter implements Runnable {
    private final boolean printEven;
    private final int limit;

    public NumberPrinter(boolean printEven, int limit) {
        this.printEven = printEven;
        this.limit = limit;
    }

    @Override
    public void run() {
        String name = Thread.currentThread().getName();
        int start = printEven ? 2 : 1;
        for (int i = start; i <= limit; i += 2) {
            System.out.printf("[%s] %s: %d\n", name, (printEven ? "EVEN" : "ODD"), i);
            try {
                Thread.sleep(50); // Yield CPU to demonstrate concurrent interleaving
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                System.err.println(name + " interrupted");
            }
        }
        System.out.println("[" + name + "] Finished Task.");
    }
}

public class ConcurrentThreadsDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("=== Main Thread Initialized ===");

        // Create tasks
        Runnable oddTask = new NumberPrinter(false, 10);
        Runnable evenTask = new NumberPrinter(true, 10);

        // Wrap in Thread objects
        Thread t1 = new Thread(oddTask, "Odd-Worker");
        Thread t2 = new Thread(evenTask, "Even-Worker");

        // Start threads concurrently
        t1.start();
        t2.start();

        try {
            // Main thread calls join() to ensure it finishes last
            t1.join();
            t2.join();
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            System.err.println("Main thread interrupted during join: " + e.getMessage());
        }

        System.out.println("=== All Worker Threads Terminated. Main Thread Exiting. ===");
    }
}
```

$$\therefore \text{Implementing Runnable enables clean object reuse, concurrency, and thread pool scaling.}$$
